{
  "subject": {
    "slug": "patient-care",
    "title": "Patient Care",
    "order": 4
  },
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "equipment-monitoring-emergencies",
    "title": "Equipment, Monitoring & Emergencies",
    "order": 0,
    "pages": [
      {
        "title": "Why This Matters for RTTs",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "As a radiation therapist, you're not just operating equipment \u2014 you're often the only clinical staff member with a patient during simulation and daily treatment. Recognizing abnormal vital signs, managing common tubes and lines safely, and responding to a medical emergency are core parts of the job, not someone else's responsibility."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "This lesson covers vital signs, oxygen therapy, chest tubes and lines, and the basics of CPR \u2014 then hands you off to the Vital Signs Simulator for hands-on practice."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The Vital Signs",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Vital signs are objective, non-invasive measurements that can be taken quickly: "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "temperature, pulse, respiration rate, and blood pressure",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " are the core four, with oxygen saturation now considered essential in most clinical settings."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Vital signs assessment should be fast and objective \u2014 it's a snapshot, not a diagnosis. A single abnormal reading is a cue to look closer and reassess, not necessarily an emergency by itself."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Temperature and Respiration",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Body temperature",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " abnormalities: "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "hypothermia",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " is a temperature below 97.7\u00b0F; "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "hyperthermia",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " (fever) is an oral temperature above 99.5\u00b0F."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Respiration",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " is the rhythmic exchange of gases through the lungs, measured as breaths per minute. Key abnormalities:"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "bulletList",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Tachypnea",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 rapid breathing, greater than 20 breaths/min"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Bradypnea",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 abnormally slow respiratory rate"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Dyspnea",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 difficult or labored breathing"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Orthopnea",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 difficulty breathing unless sitting or standing upright"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Apnea",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 absence of spontaneous breathing"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Accurate assessment means tracking rate, depth, "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "and",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "italic"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " pattern \u2014 not just a number."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Pulse and Blood Pressure",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Pulse",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " reflects the heart's contractions transmitted through the arterial walls. Common measurement sites: "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "radial",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " (wrist), "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "brachial",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " (inner elbow \u2014 common in infants), and "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "carotid",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " (neck \u2014 the standard site during CPR)."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Normal adult pulse: 60\u2013100 bpm. "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Tachycardia",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " is above 100 bpm; "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "bradycardia",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " is an abnormally low rate. Children run faster: 70\u2013120 bpm (ages 1\u201310), 100\u2013160 bpm (newborns)."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Blood pressure",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " is measured in mmHg over the brachial artery. Normal is roughly 120/80: systolic (contraction) under 120, diastolic (relaxation) under 80. "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Hypotension",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " is below normal; "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "hypertension",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " is above \u2014 often called the \"silent killer\" since patients are frequently asymptomatic."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "knowledge_check",
            "key": "lq_page4_1",
            "stem": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "During adult CPR, pulse is typically checked at which site?"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "options": [
              "Radial",
              "Carotid",
              "Brachial"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explanation": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "The carotid artery is the standard adult CPR pulse-check site, assessed for no more than 10 seconds."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Oxygen Therapy Basics",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Oxygen is essential for cellular metabolism, makes up 21% of atmospheric gas, and is "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "legally treated as a drug",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " \u2014 it must be ordered by a physician, not administered at a therapist's discretion."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Oxygen is not flammable itself, but it strongly "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "supports combustion",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " \u2014 a real fire-safety consideration around electrical equipment."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Oxygen flow is measured in "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "liters per minute (LPM)",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": ". The universal color for oxygen equipment is "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "green",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " \u2014 the flowmeter is green and labeled \"OXYGEN.\""
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Oxygen Delivery Devices",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Devices fall into two broad categories:"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "bulletList",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Low-flow devices",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 nasal cannula, simple masks"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "High-flow devices",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 aerosol masks, non-rebreathing masks, tents/oxyhoods, ventilators"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "If transporting a patient on portable oxygen, check the supply "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "before",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "italic"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " transport to make sure there's enough for the trip plus the full procedure \u2014 and secure the tank so it can't roll or fall."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "knowledge_check",
            "key": "lq_page6_1",
            "stem": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "A patient arrives on oxygen via nasal cannula for a simulation scan. What's the correct action regarding the oxygen device?"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "options": [
              "Remove it temporarily to get a cleaner scan, then replace it",
              "Never remove it without the consent/supervision of a physician, respiratory therapist, or nurse"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explanation": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "An oxygen device should never be completely removed for the sake of a procedure without appropriate medical consent or supervision."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Chest Tubes, Central Lines, and Airway Tubes",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "You'll encounter patients with a range of tubes and lines, especially in inpatient or post-surgical settings:"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "bulletList",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Endotracheal (ET) tubes",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 placed in the trachea for mechanical ventilation or oxygen delivery"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Chest tubes (thoracostomy)",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 drain the pleural space or mediastinum. "
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Never clamp a chest drain unless specifically ordered",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": ", and never lift the drainage unit above chest level (this can cause fluid/air to flow back toward the patient)."
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Central venous pressure (CVP) lines",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 inserted into a major vein for medication administration, fluid management, blood draws/transfusions, and monitoring cardiac pressures"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Pulmonary arterial lines",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 have a balloon tip that floats and wedges into the pulmonary artery for pressure monitoring"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "The common thread: these lines exist for a medical reason you may not know the details of. When in doubt, don't disconnect, clamp, or reposition \u2014 ask."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Other Equipment You'll Assist With",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Beyond oxygen and IV-type lines, you'll routinely assist patients with:"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "bulletList",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Nasogastric (NG) tubes",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 for feeding or decompression; be aware of them during transfers and positioning"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Urinary catheters, urinals, and bedpans",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 maintaining dignity and proper drainage bag positioning (below bladder level) matters during setup"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Infusion pumps",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 keep the pump upright and be mindful of tubing length/tension when moving or positioning a patient"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "The general principle across all of these: know what's attached to your patient "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "before",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "italic"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " you move them, and never disconnect something to make positioning \"easier.\""
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Medical Emergencies and CPR Basics",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "If a patient goes into cardiac arrest, the sequence is: verify (check responsiveness and breathing, assess carotid pulse for no more than 10 seconds) \u2192 call for help/activate emergency response and get the AED \u2192 begin CPR immediately."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Adult CPR:",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " 100\u2013120 compressions/minute, 2 inches (5 cm) depth, ratio of 30 compressions to 2 breaths (one-person). In two-person CPR, one person compresses while the other ventilates, same 30:2 ratio."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Child CPR",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " (ages 1\u201312): compression depth about 1/3 the chest diameter (~2 in). One-person ratio 30:2; two-person ratio 15:2."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Infant CPR",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " (under 1 year): two or three fingers at the center of the chest just below the nipple line, depth about 1.25 inches. One-person ratio 30:2; two-person ratio 15:2."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "knowledge_check",
            "key": "lq_page9_1",
            "stem": {
              "type": "doc",
